Given an integer array nums, return the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.

Example 1:
Input: nums = [10,9,2,5,3,7,101,18]
Output: 4
Explanation: The longest increasing subsequence is [2,3,7,101], therefore the length is 4.

Example 2:
Input: nums = [0,1,0,3,2,3]
Output: 4

Example 3:
Input: nums = [7,7,7,7,7,7,7]
Output: 1

Constraints:
1 <= nums.length <= 2500
-104 <= nums[i] <= 104

Approach 1:
In the first approach we take two indexes last and curr index. The last to keep a track of the last index we picked
(if picked or else it will have the initialisation value i.e. -1) then the curr to keep track of the curr element we are
at. If the condition nums[lastIndex] < nums [currIndex] is met then the element is picked meaning, we increase the count
by 1 and the remaining part is solved recursively. Also, a not pick part runs whether the condition is met or not,
recursively we move the curr and check. The max out of the two will be returned. As there is a usage of -1 and with
respect to the base case we need a +1 increase in the dp dimensions during memo and tab. The -1 usage would lead to a
shifting in the column values by 1.

Note:
We can also do this in the reverse direction where we would run a loop for every element considered as the end of the LIS
and keep moving two pointers again in the same pick and not pick fashion of the approach 1 in the reverse direction and
find the max. The recursive naive version of this is present in the solution. The time complexity for the naive solution
will still be 2^n as the loop does a constant work where for every iteration of the loop up to 2^n work is done hence an
addition of 2^0 + 2^1 + ... + 2^n = 2^n takes place.

Naive approach -> TC = O(2^n), SC = O(n) (recursion stack space)
Memoization -> TC = O(n^2), SC = O(n^2) + O(n) (recursion stack space) = O(n^2)
Tabulation -> TC = O(n^2), SC = O(n^2)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_1 -> TC = O(n^2), SC = O(2 * n) = O(n)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_2 -> TC = O(n^2), SC = O(n)

Approach 2:
In the second approach we solve this problem by seeing the problem as what is the LIS if each element is considered
as the end of any valid candidate for LIS. So we find the LIS for every element of the array considering it to be the
end of the LIS. This way we only have to maintain a single state in the recursion. We run two loops where the first one
moves considering every element as last part of the LIS and the loop takes the max out of all. The second loop that runs
inside the recursive function checking for the next best part of the LIS (the recursive sub problems solve for the 2nd
last element then the next call for the 3rd last element and so on) going only till i and by taking the max in the loop.
If there is an element satisfying the condition nums[j] < nums[i] then 1 + f(j) is taken, else if the condition is not
met then the default value is 1 since the current element contributes it's length to the current LIS candidate. The second
loop also takes care of the base case hence there is no need of a separate base case. The memo and tab versions become
simple to write and this approach is also used in other problems.

Note:
1. Here the naive approach comes out to have a TC of 2^n because for every i the j loop works double it had the last time
so from the reduction of the recurrence relation we see a series of 2^0 + 2^1 + ... + 2^n = 2^n work being done hence
the complexity.

2. Print the LIS. Add a parent[i] array that records which j gave dp[i] its value (set it whenever currLength improves, and
use parent[i] = i or -1 when nums[i] is on its own). Then start from the index with the largest dp, and follow parent back
to rebuild the sequence (in reverse).

Naive approach -> TC = O(2^n), SC = O(n) (recursion stack space)
Memoization -> TC = O(n^2), SC = O(n) + O(n) (recursion stack space) = O(n)
Tabulation -> TC = O(n^2), SC = O(n)

